                 (all GTK-free except page-identity, in libpipnode-core),
                 lib/pn-mesh-{dialog,device-list,page-identity}.{c,h}
                 (libpipnode-gui).
                 Unit tests: test-pn-mesh-pb (123 checks), test-pn-mesh-frame
                 (44 checks). Firmware version + HW model name deferred to
                 Phase 3 (needs admin protocol round-trip).
                 User-verified against real Heltec V3.
    [x] Phase 3  Owner-names write (smallest write path), admin
                 protocol primitives, DeviceMetadata read. Long/short
                 name editable on Identity with per-field Apply
                 (write -> 0.5s settle -> verifying handshake);
                 Identity also shows firmware version, hardware model
                 with friendly name (HELTEC_V3 etc.), device role,
                 capability flags (Wi-Fi / Bluetooth / Ethernet). New
                 admin-protocol helpers in pn-mesh-connection.c wrap
                 MeshPacket+Data+AdminMessage so Phase 4+ can
                 piggyback on send_admin/parse_admin_message for
                 set_lora_config etc. HW-model + role lookup tables
                 ported from pip-mesh inline in pn-mesh-page-identity.c.
                 GOTCHAS rediscovered: (i) the page status callback
                 signature is (text, user_data) -- DO NOT
                 function-cast set_status which is (ctx, text), the
                 args swap and crash on first emit; bridge through a
                 small thunk. (ii) After a successful Apply, only
                 repaint the entry whose write we just finished --
                 the other entry may hold a mid-edit value the user
                 has typed but not applied, and a blind repaint
                 silently throws it away. Tracked via an "applying"
                 enum in the page ctx; APPLY_NONE on a fresh device
                 pick paints both, APPLY_LONG/SHORT after Apply
                 paints just that one. User-verified.
    [x] Phase 4  Region / LoRa page. Region picker, modem preset,
                 hop limit, tx_power, tx_enabled, single Apply
                 button. Extended handshake captures FromRadio.config
                 (field 5) -> Config { lora (6) = LoRaConfig {...} }.
                 New pn_mesh_connection_set_lora_config_async wraps
                 AdminMessage.set_config (34) = Config(lora=...) and
                 ships the WHOLE LoRaConfig at once (proto3 defaults
                 would silently reset omitted fields -- region=UNSET
                 would brick the radio). channel_num + use_preset are
                 read and round-tripped verbatim. Dialog right-pane
                 now hosts a GtkStackSwitcher above the GtkStack;
                 Identity + Region pages are titled (appear as
                 switcher buttons); empty/connecting page stays
                 untitled. Switcher hidden until a device connects.
                 Files shipped: lib/pn-mesh-page-region.{c,h}; deltas
                 in lib/pn-mesh-connection.{c,h} (LoRa fields +
                 parse_config + set_lora_config) and lib/pn-mesh-
                 dialog.c. User-verified against real Heltec V3.
    [x] Phase 5  Channels page. List of 8 slots (index/name/role
                 badge/PSK lock icon), Add channel (modal with name +
                 prefilled random AES-256 PSK + Generate button),
                 per-row Delete (primary protected, disabled slots
                 inert). Bare set_channel admin writes (no
                 begin/commit transaction; that was Phase 5 plan but
                 single-channel writes don't need it -- pip-mesh's
                 create_channel/delete_channel pattern). New
                 pn_mesh_connection_set_channel_sync/async with
                 AdminMessage.set_channel (field 33, NOT 32 -- the
                 pip-mesh docstring at line 3905 says 32 but the
                 code at line 3941 uses 33; trust the code). Spinner
                 next to Add button while the 3-5s verify cycle
                 runs. Files shipped: lib/pn-mesh-page-channels.{c,h}
                 plus deltas in pn-mesh-connection / pn-mesh-serial /
                 pn-mesh-dialog. User-verified against real Heltec V3.

                 LOAD-BEARING GOTCHAS rediscovered, also relevant to
                 Phase 6/7 (any future set_channel-shaped write):

                 (i) PORT BOUNCE FOR set_channel. The Meshtastic
                 firmware buffers channel-slot changes in RAM and
                 only commits to flash when the serial session ends
                 (DTR drop). pip-mesh gets this for free because it
                 uses `echo > $tty` (a fresh open/close per write);
                 our long-lived fd has to close+reopen explicitly or
                 the write LOOKS accepted (verify-cycle handshake
                 succeeds) but is silently dropped on the next
                 device read or reboot, and pip-mesh later sees no
                 change. set_owner and set_lora_config are stored
                 differently and do NOT need the bounce. Added
                 pn_mesh_serial_reopen() and the connection now
                 bounces the fd between the set_channel write and
                 the verify cycle. Phase 6's QR-import path will
                 write multiple channels in sequence -- bundle them
                 in a single begin_edit_settings(64)/commit_edit_
                 settings(65) transaction (per pip-mesh's
                 --copy-channels) and one bounce at the end.

                 (ii) use-after-free across the modal-destroy in the
                 Add dialog: gtk_entry_get_text returns a pointer
                 INTO the entry's buffer; destroying the dialog
                 frees the entry. The async write's internal
                 g_strdup runs AFTER the destroy, picking up freed
                 memory -- the channel name comes back as garbage on
                 the next read-back. Fix: g_strdup the entry text
                 BEFORE destroying the dialog. (psk_bytes was
                 already safe because decode_psk_hex mallocs a fresh
                 buffer we own.)

                 (iii) UX: a 3-5s verify cycle with no visible
                 motion reads as "the app froze". Add a GtkSpinner
                 next to the action button, spin while writing=TRUE.
                 Identity (Apply owner) and Region (Apply LoRa) have
                 the same delay and would benefit from the same
                 spinner -- not added yet (user didn't ask), one-
                 liner each when wanted.
    [x] Known    Known Nodes page (out-of-band insertion between
        Nodes    Phase 5 and Phase 6 -- user-requested "everything
                 works" diagnostic).  Read-only sortable
                 GtkTreeView over PnMeshState->nodes; the device's
                 own row is bolded and pinned to the top via a
