    [x] Phase 12 Position + Power config tabs.  Shipped as two
                 new GtkExpanders distributed across the existing
                 7-tab layout rather than dedicated tabs -- Power
                 sits under "Device" alongside Identity, Position
                 sits under "Radio" after Region / Channels / Share.
                 Both reuse the LoRa pattern: read whole sub-block
                 during the handshake, mutate one or more fields,
                 ship every typed field back at once via
                 AM_SET_CONFIG=34 wrapped in Config{<sub>}.

                 Wire side (pn-mesh-connection.[ch]): CFG_POSITION
                 =2 and CFG_POWER=3 dispatch in parse_config; POS_*
                 / POW_* field defines mirror the Meshtastic proto;
                 parse_position_config / parse_power_config follow
                 parse_lora_config's shape.  PnMeshState gains 13
                 pos_* and 9 pow_* fields plus have_position /
                 have_power flags.  Two new writer pairs --
                 PnMeshPositionConfigWrite + set_position_config_*,
                 PnMeshPowerConfigWrite + set_power_config_* --
                 share two helpers (send_set_config_subblock and
                 finish_set_config) extracted from set_lora_config
                 so the second writer collapses to a per-field
                 write loop + one call.  Power's
                 adc_multiplier_override is the project's first
                 float-on-the-wire field; written via
                 pn_mesh_pb_write_fixed32_field with a union punning
                 trick, read via pn_mesh_pb_read_float.

                 UI side: pn-mesh-page-position.[c,h] and
                 pn-mesh-page-power.[c,h] are direct clones of
                 pn-mesh-page-region.[c,h]: subtitle + grid of
                 bold-label / control rows + right-aligned Apply
                 button at the bottom; ctx struct with all widget
                 handles + status / busy callbacks + writing flag;
                 set_writing toggles every control's sensitivity in
                 one shot and emits busy(TRUE/FALSE) on transition;
                 set_state mirrors the device state into the
                 controls and stashes round-trip-only fields
                 (position_flags, the GPIOs, the legacy gps_enabled
                 vs gps_mode pair, the INA address) in ctx so Apply
                 ships them back unchanged.

                 Position page exposes GPS mode (combo: DISABLED /
                 ENABLED / NOT_PRESENT), the legacy gps_enabled
                 boolean synced from mode at Apply-time so older
                 firmware keeps consistent state, fixed_position
                 switch, smart-broadcast switch + its min-distance
                 (m) and min-interval (s) thresholds, plus
                 position_broadcast_secs and gps_update_interval.
                 GPIO assignments are deliberately NOT surfaced as
                 controls -- they are board-specific and the user
                 is more likely to set them via the firmware
                 console than the dialog; round-trip preserves
                 them anyway.

                 Power page exposes is_power_saving switch,
                 on_battery_shutdown_after_secs,
                 adc_multiplier_override (float spinner, 3-digit
                 precision, 0 = firmware default),
                 wait_bluetooth_secs, sds_secs, ls_secs,
                 min_wake_secs; device_battery_ina_address is
                 round-tripped only.  powermon_enables (field 32)
                 is a debug-only flag the dialog ignores -- same
                 caveat the LoRa page carries for spread_factor
                 etc.  It gets proto3-zero-reset on Apply (rare;
                 no end-user touched it via the GUI), document
                 if a user reports it.

                 Gotchas for the next Config sub-block writer
                 (Phase 14):

                 (i) Float on the wire uses
                 pn_mesh_pb_write_fixed32_field with a union to
                 reinterpret bits; pn_mesh_pb_read_float
                 reciprocates.  Do NOT write a varint -- proto's
                 float wire type is fixed32 (wire 5), and a varint
                 of the bit pattern would be read as garbage.

                 (ii) Signed int32 fields on the wire encode
                 negative values as 10-byte sign-extended varints.
                 The dialog's smart-min-distance spinner clamps to
                 0 so the path is never hit in practice, but the
                 writer still casts gint32 -> gint64 -> guint64
                 before pn_mesh_pb_write_varint_field so the
                 contract is correct for any signed field added
                 later.

                 (iii) The pattern is: parse all fields the
                 firmware sends, expose only the practically-
                 useful ones in the UI, stash the rest in ctx and
                 ship them back unchanged.  This avoids the
                 proto3-defaults trap and keeps board-specific /
                 advanced settings intact without bloating the
                 page.

