(33) Meshtastic discovery: stop treating a BARE 10c4:ea60 as a Meshtastic
device.  The VID:PID table in lib/pn-mesh-discover.c (KNOWN_DEVICES) matches
the Heltec V3 on 10c4:ea60 -- but that is the GENERIC Silicon Labs CP2102 /
CP2102N USB-UART chip, used by countless unrelated boards.  In particular the
common Sonoff/ITead Zigbee dongles (ZBDongle-P) are CP2102N -> 10c4:ea60, so a
plugged-in Zigbee coordinator shows up in the Meshtastic dialog as a "Heltec
V3".  Picking it aims the Meshtastic want_config_id handshake at a Z-Stack
UART (root cause of the 2026-05-29 zigbee2mqtt CPU-spin incident: pipnode
opened the shared /dev/ttyUSB and wrote Meshtastic frames into it; zigbee2mqtt
choked on the corrupted byte stream).

  MITIGATED 2026-05-29 (see the in-use guard added the same day): such a
  device, while it is open by zigbee2mqtt (or anything else), now appears
  DISABLED in the list with an "in use by <proc>" note and can never be
  opened (pn_mesh_tty_in_use + TIOCEXCL in pn-mesh-serial.c).  But that only
  helps WHILE the other process holds the port -- an idle, unclaimed CP2102
  Zigbee dongle would still be mislabelled "Heltec V3" and be connectable.

  THE WORK (why it is hard): sysfs alone cannot disambiguate -- the product /
  manufacturer / serial strings are the chip's generic defaults
  ("CP2102 USB to UART Bridge Controller" / "Silicon Labs"), identical on a
  Heltec and a Zigbee dongle.  The only reliable tell is a safe, read-only
  probe over the wire (e.g. a Meshtastic want_config_id and a bounded wait for
  a valid 0x94 0xC3 frame) -- but ANY write is exactly what we must NOT do to
  a non-Meshtastic device.  Options to weigh: (a) mark generic-CP2102 matches
  as "unverified / possibly another device" in the UI and require an explicit
  user confirm before the first write; (b) keep a denylist of known-NON-mesh
  CP2102 product/serial strings; (c) cross-check against a system Zigbee/serial
  registry.  None is clean; deferred deliberately.

(34) Device-dialog toolkit + PnDeviceProvider (was TODO #29 Phase 8).
The Devices -> Meshtastic dialog (lib/pn-mesh-dialog.c,
